# Generic tooling and LuaUnit migration plan

## Objective

Replace SoulSearch's existing `tools/` scripts with the reusable toolchain
currently staged under `NewTools/`, migrate the complete pure-Lua test suite
from the custom `tests/support/testlib.lua` framework to LuaUnit, and preserve
SoulSearch's source, package, and live DFHack behavior through the cutover.

The new `Build.ps1` behavior is the target contract. A default build must:

- syntax-check production and test Lua with `luac`;
- load optional machine-local configuration from `.env.local`;
- resolve the mod ID from `src/info.txt`;
- run `<mod-id> reload` through `dfhack-run.exe`; and
- fail when either syntax validation or the live reload fails.

`Publish.ps1` may deliberately call the build with `-LiveReload:$false` while
constructing a package. That explicit publish-only override does not change the
default interactive build contract.

## Architectural decisions

- Keep reusable mechanics in `tools/`; move SoulSearch-specific assertions into
  SoulSearch tests instead of embedding them in generic packaging scripts.
- Keep LuaUnit pinned to `3.5-1` in the repository-local `.luarocks/` tree.
- Discover test files by the existing `test_*.lua` / `*_test.lua` convention;
  support modules, fixtures, and `tests/run.lua` must not be treated as suites.
- Make `tests/run.lua` derive the repository root from its own location instead
  of interpreting the first LuaUnit argument as a path.
- Replace the copied `DOTHIS_TEST_FILES` name with a project-neutral runner
  contract. No copied script may retain DoThis or another sibling project's
  name, paths, commands, or package assumptions.
- Preserve test behavior and coverage during framework conversion. Passing
  fewer assertions or silently omitting a suite is not a successful migration.
- Keep source/package truth anchored to `src/`, including root `info.txt` and
  `scripts_modinstalled/`.
- Perform the final `NewTools/` to `tools/` replacement atomically after the new
  entrypoints and migrated test runner are proven together.

## Non-goals

- Do not change SoulSearch production behavior merely to simplify tests.
- Do not weaken DFHack stubs, module isolation, UI characterization, package
  payload checks, or live smoke requirements.
- Do not make LuaUnit or `.luarocks/` part of the published mod payload.
- Do not remove live reload from the default build.
- Do not treat a successful syntax check as proof that the LuaUnit suite,
  package, or live UI is correct.

## Phase 0 - Record the existing contracts and migration baseline

☐ Inventory the old and new parameters, environment variables, external
  dependencies, generated files, exit behavior, and callers for:
  - `Build.ps1` and `Check-LuaSyntax.ps1`;
  - `Test.ps1` and `Run-UnitTests.ps1`;
  - `Publish.ps1` and `VerifyPackage.ps1`;
  - `Common.ps1`.
☐ Record that default live reload is an accepted requirement of the new
  build, including the expected `DFHACK_RUNNER`,
  `DFHACK_DWARF_FORTRESS_ROOT`, `DFHACK_LUAC`, and
  `DFHACK_LUA_VERSION` configuration seams.
☐ Run the current custom test suite and record its exact suite, case,
  assertion, and failure totals where the current runner exposes them.
☐ Run the current syntax-only build and record the exact production-file
  count and compiler version.
☐ Capture the current source manifest and the manifests of the expanded and
  zip packages, including whether the zip has a package-root directory.
☐ Record the existing project-specific package invariants currently checked
  outside generic manifest equality, including:
  - `info.txt` at the package root;
  - `scripts_modinstalled/soulsearch.lua`;
  - the `--@module=true` and `--@enable=true` annotations;
  - `internal/soulsearch/keybindings.lua`.
☐ Add the baseline results to the execution-evidence table before changing
  the active `tools/` entrypoints.

Exit gate: the pre-migration test coverage, syntax scope, package shape,
SoulSearch-specific package invariants, and live-build target are explicit and
recorded.

## Phase 1 - Harden the reusable common, syntax, and build tools

☐ Audit `NewTools/Common.ps1`, `Check-LuaSyntax.ps1`, and `Build.ps1` for
  sibling-project names, paths, IDs, commands, and assumptions; remove every
  project-specific leak.
☐ Keep the new build defaults intact, especially `LiveReload=$true`,
  `.env.local`, `src/info.txt`, and `<mod-id> reload`.
☐ Reconcile `Build.ps1 -SourceDir` with syntax checking so a caller cannot
  select one source tree for metadata while `Check-LuaSyntax.ps1` silently
  validates another. Either pass explicit search roots into the syntax checker
  or remove any parameter whose advertised behavior is not real.
☐ Make syntax-check output distinguish production files from test/support
  files and report accurate counts when `-IncludeTests` is active.
☐ Verify explicit and environment-based compiler resolution, including the
  configured Lua-version mismatch failure.
☐ Verify `.env.local` parsing for comments, optional `export`, quoted values,
  missing optional files, invalid entries, and precedence of process variables
  over file values.
☐ Verify DFHack runner resolution through an explicit path, a Dwarf Fortress
  root, and PATH, with clear failures for missing runner files.
☐ Verify reload output is printed, optionally captured to
  `ReloadOutputPath`, and preserved in the failure report when reload exits
  nonzero.
☐ Add `.env.local` to `.gitignore`; add a safe example/template only if the
  required variables cannot be documented clearly without one.

Exit gate: the reusable build stack validates the intended files, retains all
accepted defaults, contains no sibling-project leakage, and has focused proof
for configuration and failure behavior.

## Phase 2 - Establish the repo-local LuaUnit runner contract

☐ Add `.luarocks/` to `.gitignore` and confirm it cannot enter `src/` or a
  published package.
☐ Keep LuaUnit pinned to `3.5-1`; check and install that exact version into
  `.luarocks/` rather than accepting an arbitrary globally installed version.
☐ Replace `DOTHIS_TEST_FILES` with a documented project-neutral mechanism
  for passing the discovered suite list to `tests/run.lua`.
☐ Make `tests/run.lua` derive `repo_root`, `tests/`, and
  `src/scripts_modinstalled/` paths from its own location so LuaUnit CLI
  arguments remain untouched.
☐ Preserve the caller's existing `LUA_PATH` while adding the repo-local rock
  path, test support path, and production-module path; restore process
  environment variables on both success and failure.
☐ Preserve passthrough of LuaUnit CLI arguments for targeted tests, output
  modes, verbosity, and failure controls.
☐ Ensure test discovery is deterministic, selects all current suite files,
  and excludes `run.lua`, support modules, and fixtures.
☐ Avoid terminating a parent PowerShell host unexpectedly when
  `Run-UnitTests.ps1` is invoked from another tool; expose a reliable nonzero
  process result without bypassing cleanup.
☐ Add one LuaUnit smoke suite that proves dependency loading, repository-root
  resolution, discovery, and a deliberate assertion failure path.

Exit gate: `tools/Run-UnitTests.ps1` can bootstrap pinned LuaUnit and execute a
discovered smoke suite with correct paths, arguments, cleanup, and exit status.

## Phase 3 - Bridge the existing test suite onto LuaUnit

☐ Introduce the smallest temporary compatibility adapter needed to map the
  current `test.case` and assertion API onto LuaUnit without changing test
  meaning.
☐ Preserve the existing per-file registration boundary while the adapter is
  active so every current suite continues to receive a valid repository root
  and isolated harness state.
☐ Map every custom assertion with equivalent failure semantics and useful
  messages:
  - true, false, and nil;
  - exact equality;
  - numeric tolerance;
  - ordered sequence equality.
☐ Make dynamic case names visible and uniquely identifiable in LuaUnit
  output.
☐ Execute all existing `*_test.lua` suites through the LuaUnit runner and
  compare discovered file, case, and assertion totals with the Phase 0
  baseline.
☐ Deliberately fail one representative domain assertion and one harness/UI
  assertion to prove failures are neither swallowed nor reported as passes;
  revert only the deliberate failures after recording the proof.

Exit gate: the complete pre-existing suite runs under LuaUnit with no omitted
suites or weakened assertions, while production code remains unchanged.

## Phase 4 - Convert suites to native LuaUnit and retire the custom framework

☐ Convert domain/catalog/formatting suites to native LuaUnit structures and
  assertions; run the full suite after the batch.
☐ Convert candidate-provider, filter-state, preset, settings, search, and
  lifecycle suites; run the full suite after the batch.
☐ Convert module-loader, script-harness, DFHack-stub, and environment-heavy
  suites while preserving per-test isolation and global restoration; run the
  full suite after the batch.
☐ Convert UI component, overlay, pointer, tooltip, geometry, refresh, and
  characterization suites; run the full suite after the batch.
☐ Replace the explicit suite registry in `tests/run.lua` with the proven
  deterministic discovery contract.
☐ Remove the temporary compatibility adapter and
  `tests/support/testlib.lua` only after no suite requires them.
☐ Search the complete `tests/` tree for old `test.case`, custom assertion,
  suite-registration, and positional `repo_root` conventions; the result must
  be empty except for intentional migration-history text in this plan.
☐ Compare final suite/case/assertion coverage with the Phase 0 baseline and
  explain any intentional count change in the execution evidence.

Exit gate: every SoulSearch test is natively executed and reported by LuaUnit,
the custom framework is gone, and the full suite retains baseline behavior and
coverage.

## Phase 5 - Migrate publishing and package verification

☐ Audit `NewTools/Publish.ps1` and `VerifyPackage.ps1` for sibling-project
  leakage and confirm all project identity comes from `src/info.txt` or
  explicit parameters.
☐ Reconcile `Publish.ps1 -SourceDir` with its build invocation so a custom
  source selection is consistently validated, packaged, and verified.
☐ Preserve the new publish behavior that calls the build with
  `-LiveReload:$false`; publishing must not require or trigger a running game.
☐ Record and adopt one intentional zip-root contract, then make
  `Publish.ps1`, `VerifyPackage.ps1`, README installation instructions, and
  tests agree on it.
☐ Record whether the removed `NoRootFolder` and `NoExpandedFolder` options
  have any remaining callers; either preserve a generic equivalent or update
  every caller and document their removal.
☐ Preserve byte-for-byte/SHA-256 manifest comparison between `src/`, the
  expanded package, and the extracted zip, with useful missing, unexpected,
  and differing-file diagnostics.
☐ Move SoulSearch-specific bootstrap checks out of the generic verifier and
  into focused project tests so generic verification does not weaken these
  invariants:
  - required `soulsearch.lua` annotations;
  - keybinding bootstrap dependency;
  - public command and required metadata presence.
☐ Verify both repository-relative and absolute source/package paths.
☐ Verify temporary staging/extraction directories are cleaned after success
  and after forced verification failure.
☐ Verify tests, tools, `.luarocks/`, `.env.local`, docs, and planning files
  are absent from both package forms.

Exit gate: publishing is reusable and non-live, both package forms exactly
match the intended source payload, and SoulSearch-specific package guarantees
remain covered by project tests.

## Phase 6 - Perform the atomic toolchain cutover

☐ Place the finalized `Build.ps1`, `Check-LuaSyntax.ps1`, `Common.ps1`,
  `Run-UnitTests.ps1`, `Publish.ps1`, and `VerifyPackage.ps1` under `tools/`.
☐ Remove obsolete `tools/Test.ps1` references only after
  `tools/Run-UnitTests.ps1` passes the complete suite.
☐ Remove `NewTools/` only after its finalized contents are present under
  `tools/` and a manifest comparison confirms no accidental omissions.
☐ Update `.vscode/tasks.json` so Build invokes the accepted default live
  build and Unit Tests invokes `tools/Run-UnitTests.ps1`.
☐ Update `README.md` and `tests/README.md` for:
  - Lua, `luac`, LuaRocks, and DFHack runner prerequisites;
  - `.env.local` and `DFHACK_*` configuration;
  - default live build/reload behavior;
  - LuaUnit bootstrap and argument passthrough;
  - publish/package layout and installation.
☐ Update active `.todo` files and durable docs that still name
  `tools/Test.ps1` or describe the old syntax-only build contract.
☐ Search the repository for old tool names, removed parameters, DoThis
  identifiers, obsolete package-root claims, and stale command examples; fix
  every actionable hit.
☐ Preserve unrelated working-tree changes during the cutover.

Exit gate: `tools/` is the sole toolchain location, all repository callers and
documentation use the new contracts, and no copied-project leakage or stale
entrypoint remains.

## Phase 7 - Complete automated, package, and live validation

☐ From a clean PowerShell process, run `tools/Run-UnitTests.ps1` with an
  empty `.luarocks/` tree and record dependency bootstrap plus exact LuaUnit
  results.
☐ Run `tools/Run-UnitTests.ps1` again with the dependency already installed
  and prove the pinned-version check is idempotent.
☐ Run at least one targeted suite/case through forwarded LuaUnit arguments.
☐ Run the default `tools/Build.ps1` against a configured live DFHack
  instance and record:
  - compiler/version and production/test syntax counts;
  - resolved mod ID and reload command;
  - reload exit status and relevant output.
☐ Run the default build with `ReloadOutputPath` and verify the capture file
  contains the observed DFHack output.
☐ Run `tools/Publish.ps1`, then independently run
  `tools/VerifyPackage.ps1` against the generated zip and expanded directory.
☐ Compare generated manifests with `src/` and verify all exclusion and
  SoulSearch bootstrap tests are green.
☐ Run `git diff --check` and review the final diff for accidental generated
  files, package artifacts, machine-local paths, or unrelated edits.
☐ Complete a focused live fortress smoke after the build-triggered reload:
  open SoulSearch, perform a representative search/filter interaction, close
  it, and confirm no reload, module, overlay, or input regression.
☐ Record every command and result in the execution-evidence table. Keep any
  unavailable live scenario pending instead of treating automation as proof.

Exit gate: clean-shell LuaUnit, accepted default live build, package creation,
independent package verification, diff hygiene, and focused live SoulSearch
behavior are all recorded and green.

